Just to update on this, I finally found out where the difference in degree of freedom came from. It seems that measurementInvarianceCat does not recognise std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE. When this argument is removed, the results are comparable between the two methods.

On Wednesday, 9 September 2015 17:47:33 UTC-5, Sean wrote:
> Dear list,
>
> I am running multiple-group CFA with categorical data (no missing data). The model is very simple, and everything seems fine with the conventional lavaan code. Then I turn to measurementInvarianceCat function. Surprisingly, the results look different. In particular, the chi-square and degree of freedom are calculated differently. Can anyone please let me know what causes this?
>
> model <- "M =~ M1 + M2 + M3 + M4 + M5 + M6"
> config <- cfa(model, data=M3, ordered=c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5", "M6"),std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE,group="gender", parameterization="theta")
> weak <- cfa(model, data=M3, ordered=c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5", "M6"),std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE, group="gender", group.equal=c("loadings"), parameterization="theta")
> strong<-cfa(model, data=M3, ordered=c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5", "M6"),std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE, group="gender", group.equal=c("loadings", "thresholds"), parameterization="theta")
> strict <- cfa(model, data=M3, ordered=c("M1", "M2", "M3", "M4", "M5", "M6"),std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE, group="gender", group.equal=c("loadings", "thresholds", "residuals"), parameterization="theta")
