On 11/19/2015 12:24 PM, Daniel wrote:
> Hello,
> I'm starting to use lavaan for longitudinal data analysis and looked at
> the tutorials at lavaan.org <http://lavaan.org>
> <http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/growth.html>. I wonder, if it is always
> the case that the intercept has a "1 *..."
> |
>
> |i =~1*t1 +1*t2 +1*t3 +1*t4|

Yes.

> while the slope has increasing values for each time point.
> |
>
> |s =~0*t1 +1*t2 +2*t3 +3*t4|

For a linear slope, you need to have (linearly) increasing numbers,
although you can play around with the 0; you can also have something
like -2 -1 0 1

For each timepoint, following your 0/1/2/3 sequence, you assume the
following (linear) trend for the 4 timepoints:

E(t1) = 1*mean_i + 0*mean_s
E(t2) = 1*mean_i + 1*mean_s
E(t3) = 1*mean_i + 2*mean_s
E(t4) = 1*mean_i + 3*mean_s

where 'mean_i' is the mean of the random intercept, and 'mean_s' is the
mean of the random slope.

Yves.

